**Claim.** The Schwartz space $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is dense in the Sobolev space $H^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$ with respect to the $\lVert\cdot\rVert_{H^1}$-norm.

**Recall the definitions.** The Sobolev space $H^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is the completion of $C^\infty_c(\mathbb{R}^n)$ (smooth compactly supported functions) with respect to the norm

$$\lVert u\rVert_{H^1}^2 = \lVert u\rVert_{L^2}^2 + \sum_{j=1}^n \lVert \partial_j u\rVert_{L^2}^2.$$

Equivalently, $H^1(\mathbb{R}^n) = \{u\in L^2 : \partial_j u \in L^2,\ j=1,\dots,n\}$, where $\partial_j$ is taken in the distributional sense. The Schwartz space $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ consists of all $f\in C^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n)$ such that for every multi-indices $\alpha,\beta$,

$$\sup_{x\in\mathbb{R}^n} |x^\alpha \partial^\beta f(x)| < \infty.$$

**Key inclusion.** Every compactly supported smooth function is Schwartz:

$$C^\infty_c(\mathbb{R}^n)\subseteq \mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n).$$

Indeed, if $f\in C^\infty_c(\mathbb{R}^n)$, then $f$ and all its derivatives are bounded (being continuous with compact support), and $x^\alpha \partial^\beta f(x)\equiv 0$ for $|x|$ large enough (outside $\operatorname{supp} f$), so the Schwartz seminorms are all finite.

**Density argument.** By the very definition of $H^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$ as the completion of $C^\infty_c(\mathbb{R}^n)$ under $\lVert\cdot\rVert_{H^1}$, the space $C^\infty_c(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is dense in $H^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$. Concretely: for every $u\in H^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$ and every $\varepsilon>0$, there exists $\varphi\in C^\infty_c(\mathbb{R}^n)$ with

$$\lVert u - \varphi\rVert_{H^1} < \varepsilon.$$

(This can also be shown constructively via mollification: take $u\in H^1$, truncate by $\chi_R(x)=\chi(x/R)$ with $\chi\in C^\infty_c$, $\chi\equiv 1$ on $B_1$, so that $\chi_R u\to u$ in $H^1$ as $R\to\infty$ by dominated convergence; then mollify $\chi_R u$ with $\rho_\delta\in C^\infty_c$ to obtain $\rho_\delta * (\chi_R u)\in C^\infty_c$ converging to $\chi_R u$ in $H^1$ as $\delta\to 0$.)

Since $C^\infty_c(\mathbb{R}^n)\subseteq \mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)\subseteq H^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$, the approximant $\varphi$ above is in fact a Schwartz function. Therefore, for every $u\in H^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$ and every $\varepsilon>0$, there exists $\varphi\in\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ with $\lVert u-\varphi\rVert_{H^1}<\varepsilon$.

**Conclusion.** $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is dense in $H^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$ with respect to the $\lVert\cdot\rVert_{H^1}$-norm. $\blacksquare$

